My research focuses on the birational geometry of algebraic varieties. I am particularly interested in the birational geometry of irregular varieties and of linear systems on abelian varieties, in the birational geometry of surfaces and 3-folds and in natural questions on the birational geometry of higher dimensional varieties which arise in the context of the minimal model program. 

\vskip.3cm
\noindent
{\bf Questions closely connected to the minimal model program:}
There are 3 main conjectures in the minimal model program: the existence of flips, the termination of flips and abundance. In recent joint work with J. M$^c$Kernan, following ideas of Shokurov, we have shown that the existence of flips in dimension $n$ follows from the termination of flips in dimension $n-1$. We hope to apply a similar approach to the following well known conjecture\footnote{It appears that Y.T. Siu has obtained significant results towards this conjecture. To the best of my knowledge, there is no preprint available on Siu's work regarding this conjecture and I do not know what the statements of his results are.}: 
\begin{conj}\label{C} Let $(X,\Delta )$ be a $\mathbb Q$-factorial kawamata log terminal pair.
If $(K_X+\Delta)$ is big, then the ring $$\mathfrak {R}=\oplus _{n\in \mathbb{N}}H^0(X,\OO _X(n(K_X+\Delta ))$$ is finitely generated.
\end{conj} 
As in our proof of the existence of flips, the plan would be to restrict the ring $\mathfrak {R}$ to an appropriate divisor $S$ and then show that the restricted algebra is finitely generated. 
There are of course significant technical difficulties. First of all, one may no longer assume that $-(K_X+\Delta )$ is nef and big. However, this assumption was only used to extend certain sections from a divisor to the ambient variety (via the use of the Kawamata-Viehweg vanishing theorem). We plan to replace this part of the argument by the use of our extension theorem for pluri-canonical log-forms (see [24] and [26]).
A second and more problematic technical issue, is that we may no longer assume that $-S$ is ample as in the case of flips. This means that once one shows that $\mathfrak {R}_S$ is finitely generated, it is not immediately clear that it follows that $\mathfrak {R}$ is also finitely generated. We hope to solve this by using techniques similar to the proof of the non-vanishing theorem.


It is then possible that \eqref{C} would imply termination of flips directly (at least when $K_X+\Delta$ is big). This seems do deserve further investigation and to be unknown to the experts in this field.
There are also other approaches due to Shokurov to the termination of flips.
For example, when $K_X+\Delta$ is big, Birkhar has shown that termination of flips is implied by
a conjecture known as the ascending chain condition for minimal log discrepancies. It turns out that this is closely connected to the following conjecture of Borisov:
\begin{conj} For any integer $n>0$ and any real number $\epsilon >0$, the family of all complex normal projective varieties with $-K_X$ ample, $\mathbb Q$-Cartier and $\epsilon $ log terminal is bounded.
\end{conj}
It is well known that it suffices to bound the top self intersection
$(-K_X)^n$. This has been achieved by J. M$^c$Kernan under the additional
(rather strong) hypothesis that $X$ is of bounded index (i.e. there exists
a fixed integer $r>0$ such that $rK_X$ is Cartier).
One possible strategy, would be to produce effective $\Q$-divisors 
$G_x\sim _\Q -\lambda K_X$ 
with an isolated center of log canonical singularity
at a general point $x\in X$ and with $0<\lambda <1/2$.
It is then clear that for general $x,y\in X$, the divisor
$G_x+G_y$ has disconnected locus of log canonical
singularities contradicting a result of Shokurov and Koll\'ar. If $(-K_X)^n$ is sufficiently big, it is easy
to produce centers of log canonical singularities $V$ of
 $G\sim _\Q -\lambda K_X$ through a general point
$x\in X$ with $0<\lambda \ll 1$.
Applying Kawamata's subadjunction Theorem, one sees that
$(1-\lambda )K_X|V\sim K_V+B$ for an appropriate
pseudo-effective divisor $B$. Since $(1-\lambda )>0$,
one sees that $-(K_V+B)$ is ample and it makes sense to try
and proceed inductively. The problem is now to show that
the volume of $-(K_V+B)$ is bounded from below. This is in general not true,
but we hope that the additional hypothesis on the log-discrepancies
of $X$ give a way of controlling the volume of $-(K_V+B)$.

\vskip.3cm
\noindent {\bf The birational geometry of $3$-folds:}
There are many beautiful results in the literature regarding Gorenstein minimal 3-folds of general type. Recently, following work of Tsuji, Hacon-M$^c$Kernan and Takayama, it appears that these results may generalize to almost all non-Gorenstein $3$-folds of general type. For example, my student G. Todorov is working on showing that: {\it For all but finitely many families of $3$-folds of general type, one has $P_2>0$ and $|5K_X|$ induces a birational map.} I am also interested in seeing if there are positive constants $A,B$ such that for any $3$-fold of general type, one has $P_2(X)\geq AK_X^3-B$. One application of an inequality of the above form would then be to generalize the results on the boundedness of the degree of pluricanonical maps of Gorenstein minimal $3$-folds of general type to the case of index grater than $1$.  


\vskip.3cm
\noindent {\bf The geometry of irregular varieties:} 
One of the most interesting open questions in this area is the following conjecture due to Ueno:

{\bf Conjecture K.} 
{\it Let $X$ be a smooth projective variety with $\kappa (X)=0$
and let $a:X\to A$ be its Albanese morphism. Then 
(1) $a$ is an algebraic fiber space and 
(2) Let $F$ be a general fiber of $a$. Then $\kappa (F)=0$. }

(1) was answered affirmatively by Y. Kawamata.
In joint work with Alfred Chen, we have obtained various results
towards (2) of this conjecture.

I am particularly interested in the possibility of using the analytic methods developed by Siu to attack this problem. One possible approach would be to show
that: {\it Given any section of $H^0(X,\omega _X^N)$ which deforms
to first order in a direction $v \in H^1(X,\OO _X)\cong T(Pic ^0(X))$
then it deforms to arbitrarily high order.} It would then follow, using the theory of Fourier Mukai transforms, that if $P_N(X)=1$ and $\kappa (X)=0$, then $a_*(\omega _X^{\ot N})=\OO _A$ and in particular $P_N (F)=1$ as required.

A related approach would be to study pushforwards of sheaves of the form $\omega _X\otimes L\otimes {\mathcal{I}}(h)$
where $X$ is a smooth projective variety, $L$ is a pseudo-effective line bundle on $X$ and $h$ is a singular hermitian metric on $L$. It seems plausible that $f_*(\omega _X\otimes L\otimes {\mathcal{I}}(h))$ might retain some of the Hodge-theoretic properties enjoyed by higher direct images of dualizing sheaves (or more generally by higher direct images of 
sheaves of the form $\omega _X \ot L\ot \III (||L||)$ for any line bundle $L$ with $\kappa (L)\geq 0$).

Some of the other questions regarding irregular varieties that I plan to pursue are:

\noindent
(1) A conjecture of Luo and Zhang: {\it Let $X$ be a smooth projective variety of general type, then for any $\omega\in H^0(\Omega ^1_X)$ one has $Zeroes(\omega )\ne 
\emptyset$.} 

\noindent
(2) A conjecture of Zhang: {\it Let $X$ be a K\"ahler manifold such that $-K_X$ is nef, then 
the albanese map of $X$ is surjective.}




